A||||29||sloth85GT||***Questions for V8 Fiero owners......***||||5||
Z||000000||sloth85GT||12-07-2001||12:09 AM||||Hello guys and gals, I have a few questions for you. So here's the scoop, my 85GT is parked, not only cause it's winter, but because I have the "death knock", a spun rod bearing. So my trusty 2.8 is pretty much toast. I'm considering an LT1 swap with Archies kit if I get the bank loan for it. The mechanic is saying that it's not exactly a stock LT1, so it could possibly have about 400 horses. Sooooooo here lies the problems......<P>1) Tranny issues: Which Transmission would be the strongest to hold 350 to 400 horses. I have a 4 speed right now, and I've heard that'll hold up to 400HP. Is that true? If not, what transmission would be best suited for durability with that kinda power. I would like to keep a manual if possible.<P>2) Clutch issues: Does Archies kit come with one? Probably not, so what kinda clutch would handle it and how long will it last, again, durability is of key importancy.<P>3) Frame strength: Basically is there any frame problems involved aside from needing a rear sway bar to keep the rear end from being squirelly?<P>If anyone also feels the need to tell me something that is of utter importance, feel free to do so, I'm gonna need all of the info I can get. Thanks to everyone in advance.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">     <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||Archie||12-07-2001||12:37 AM||||There is another LT1 thread going on right now that can answer most of your questions. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016105.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016105.html</A> <P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000002||sloth85GT||12-07-2001||01:19 AM||||Thanks Arch. If anything, you'd be the guy that'd know all. I forgot that the LT1 had to be moved over 2". That thread reminded me of that. But as far as trannies, clutches and the frame strength, I need to know more. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||stingant0||12-07-2001||01:28 AM||||I'm not sure about the Getrag but the Muncie 4 spd is probably to weak. I know that the Dodge guys are running massive amounts of torque threw their trannies. Over 400 ft pounds. This is the SAME INTERNAL componets of the fiero Getrag. <A HREF="http://www.thedodgegarage.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thedodgegarage.com/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MinnGreenGT||12-07-2001||09:53 AM||||I know that Archie's daily driver car (a V8 Finale- I believe) has a 5 speed Izuzu tranny (supposedly the "weaker" tranny).  I recall his story that almost immediately after completing the install, he discoverd a small crack in the tranny housing... with a slow leak.  He's been driving the car for more than a year now (just adding more fluid every now and then) and has had no troubles at all!<P>As to what comes with the kit... depends on which you buy!  Check out the parts listings here: <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/v8fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/v8fiero.htm</A>  the page shows <I>exactly</I> what is included in each kit- and their price levels.  <P>I talked with quite a few V8 installers who suggest that the Deluxe kit is the best deal (all the little pieces that are included easily equal the extra price- especially when looking at how long & the number of trips to the local parts stores to locate all those pieces).<P>This is a quote from another thread that Archie participated in...<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Now you're saying that the Fiero stick shift transmission can't handle the power of a V-8 engine. I've been doing V-8 swaps for 15 years now. I've sold more than 1400 kits and I've built more than 160 V-8 Fieros myself. Out of the 43 V-8 Fieros that I've built for myself 40 of them used the ISUZU 5-speed. Of the 1400 kits sold more than 1200 of them were stick shift kits. EVERY one of my customers (& anyone who visits my web site) has my home address and phone number. There are more than 200 V-8 Fiero drivers on various Fiero related Internet forums. If there was a problem with stick shift Fiero transmissions being able to handle a V-8, don't you think that there would be a ground swell of V-8 Fiero kit customers joining you in your attempt to trash stick shift V-8 Fieros? By your own words you've built 2 NStar swaps and both of them were automatics. I'm here to tell you that you are wrong & that you have no basis in fact for your statements about stick shift V-8 Fieros. In fact, you were at Fierorama last year, you saw a row of no less than 15 V-8 Fieros lined up. One of them was an automatic, all the rest were stick shift cars. They had SBC engines that included 270 HP LM1's & 355 HP ZZ4's & Corvette LT1's. I would DARE YOU to tell any one of those guys that they drive "like grandma jones". Right now, I have 6 running & driving V-8 Fieros at my shop. 4 of them have the Isuzu 5-speed transmissions, one has a Getrag & one has the V-6 4-speed. I would put any one of them up against a NStar for reliability, gas mileage, performance, ease of assembly & total cost, anytime.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It was from here if you're interested: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010924-2-009429.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010924-2-009429.html</A> <P>(just a warning... that thread does turn into a bit of a flame war... just a warning!)<BR>------------------<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||12-07-2001||10:34 AM||||From what I've heard, the Muncie 4-speed that came on GT's is the strongest, with the Getrag 5-speed next.  The Isuzu 5-speed is the weakest, and Archie has no problem using them.  <P>When Tina grenaded her tranny some time ago, it was the clutch that failed, not the transmission itself.  And that was on a 4-cylinder tranny after about half an hour of continuous burnouts from her 400HP SBC.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sloth85GT||12-07-2001||07:01 PM||||I heard that the 4 speed on my GT can take up to 400HP is that true? Also is there any clutch or frame strength issues with that much power?||||reg||1
Z||000007||California Kid||12-07-2001||07:56 PM||||I'm posting just because I don't want you to think I'm ignoring you. I can't address the issues about the strenght of you car, don't know what condition it's in, etc. Can't answer your questions regarding your transmission (although I have heard the Muncie 4 spds are strong).<P>What I can tell you is that the Getrag 5 spd will handle 400hp with no issues. My Trans has logged 90,000 miles with the V6 and another 30,000 with the V8 (it isn't babied).<P>The "sticky" issue is what clutch and pressure plate setup to go with. My setup is completely QuarterMaster, Twin (2) 7.25 inch Clutch Disk's with Metal Floater Plate. This is a "Race Application" setup that isn't cheap, setup will cost approx. $1,000. The setup doesn't slip no matter what you throw at it, period. Durability if set up right exceeds 10,000 miles, but you have to remember these are race disk's (same as used in Sprint Cars, NASCAR, Pro Drags, etc) they are not intended for 50,000 plus miles. In addition this setup requires the Flywheel to be machined for the setup, use professionals only.<P>This setup isn't for everyone, but it sure works great. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/californiakidsig_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tina||12-08-2001||01:53 PM||||The one problem you are going to run into with the 4-speed would be the gear ratios.<BR>That's why I switched to the Izusu.<BR>80 mph an hour and it's sitting around 2300rpm. Gotta love it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Versus the 4 speed.<BR>Well, let's just say around 60mph you are going to look for another gear to shift into.<P>Tina <BR>383sbc||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||12-08-2001||03:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tina:<BR><B>The one problem you are going to run into with the 4-speed would be the gear ratios.<BR>That's why I switched to the Izusu.<BR>80 mph an hour and it's sitting around 2300rpm. Gotta love it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Versus the 4 speed.<BR>Well, let's just say around 60mph you are going to look for another gear to shift into.<P>Tina <BR>383sbc</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I thought the reason you switched to the isuzu was because you scattered that 4-spd all over the street?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jimmy||12-08-2001||03:31 PM||||Anyone who knows me personally on this forum knows I drive my V8 Fiero(Archie kit) very HARD, man I am so confident in this car that I've let other people take it for a blast down the road!  I really did beat the p!ss out of that tranny just to see what it could take.  I now have 8,000 miles on my swap on a 100,000 mile Getrag with ZERO problems.  This summer I would either go dragracing or street racing almost every weekend.  One night I must have ran 15 back to back races in about two hours, I never shut the car off and never babied the tranny.  I got this on tape BTW, hehe.<P>Next summer I will have another estimated 100+HP and 50-75Ftlbs of torque.  I want this car to run low 12s.  The only thing holding me back right now is the clutch, need to find something that holds better then my DF CF clutch(this is not Archie's clutch).<P>Jim<BR>87 Gt T-top V8<BR>87 Gt<p>[This message has been edited by Jimmy (edited 12-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||03:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>I'm posting just because I don't want you to think I'm ignoring you. I can't address the issues about the strenght of you car, don't know what condition it's in, etc. Can't answer your questions regarding your transmission (although I have heard the Muncie 4 spds are strong).<P>What I can tell you is that the Getrag 5 spd will handle 400hp with no issues. <P>The "sticky" issue is what clutch and pressure plate setup to go with. My setup is completely QuarterMaster, Twin (2) 7.25 inch Clutch Disk's with Metal Floater Plate. This is a "Race Application" setup that isn't cheap, setup will cost approx. $1,000. The setup doesn't slip no matter what you throw at it, period. Durability if set up right exceeds 10,000 miles, but you have to remember these are race disk's (same as used in Sprint Cars, NASCAR, Pro Drags, etc) they are not intended for 50,000 plus miles. In addition this setup requires the Flywheel to be machined for the setup, use professionals only.<P>This setup isn't for everyone, but it sure works great. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Keith was showing us the buildup pictures of your car while it was down in aneheim, excellent job. But he thinks that your going to have some competition for the shows as soon as his Northstar is put into his orange beast, but I still think yours is better ( after all, it is my favorite color )  =-)<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000012||California Kid||12-08-2001||04:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B> Keith was showing us the buildup pictures of your car while it was down in aneheim, excellent job. But he thinks that your going to have some competition for the shows as soon as his Northstar is put into his orange beast, but I still think yours is better ( after all, it is my favorite color )  =-)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks!.....Tell Keith Hi for me! Also tell him to look a pictures I posted in General Chat this date.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Archie||12-08-2001||07:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B> <BR>I thought the reason you switched to the isuzu was because you scattered that 4-spd all over the street?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The way I understood it, Tina already was planning the 5 speed swap and did the tranny/clutch destroy thing because the 4 speed was coming out anyway.<P>As MinnGreenGT mentioned, the junk yard Isuzu that I put into my Finale last year had a crack in the case when we put it in. I've been running it since Sept. 2000. I'm going to replace it sometime this winter and I plan  to do a "Tina" to it the day before we take it apart.<P>BTW, Jimmy, we all wanna see some of your Video Please.<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mach10||12-08-2001||07:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> The way I understood it, Tina already was planning the 5 speed swap and did the tranny/clutch destroy thing because the 4 speed was coming out anyway.<P>As MinnGreenGT mentioned, the junk yard Isuzu that I put into my Finale last year had a crack in the case when we put it in. I've been running it since Sept. 2000. I'm going to replace it sometime this winter and I plan  to do a "Tina" to it the day before we take it apart.<P>BTW, Jimmy, we all wanna see some of your Video Please.<P>Archie<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>lol... I was just joking. I read the post and that she had planned the explosion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I wasn't slamming the reliability of either trans. I still don't get how it failed, though... The case split? I would think that it tried to jump a gear, but the case got in the way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I would have thought that the chain-drive would have snapped, or one of the gears have stripped itself clean... But then, I don't have all that much experience with manuals... Autos are my specialty... More so if they say "Borg-Warner BG-65" on them *shudder*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000015||KissMySSFiero||12-08-2001||09:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> <P>I'm going to replace it sometime this winter and I plan  to do a "Tina" to it the day before we take it apart.<P><BR>Archie<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>take videos.  i wanna see this when it happens.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>88FORMULA<BR>       <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>        <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||sloth85GT||12-08-2001||09:51 PM||||So basically you guys are saying go Getrag or Isuzu and get rid of the 4spd? I'm probably gonna have to use my 4spd for a little while, so will it hold if I don't bash it too hard? What's the best clutch to use for streetability. This car will pretty much be my daily driver.||||reg||1
Z||000017||artherd||12-08-2001||09:53 PM||||I belive Tina's Pressure Plate... EXPLODED! (after being friction-heated by slippage, for half an hour, mercissly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Then the rest of the tranny came apart as pressure plate shrapnel riped around.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000018||Tina||12-08-2001||10:44 PM||||<B>Oh no</B>, not this clutch thing again.<P>To quote Eric from an earlier post, this is what happend<BR><B>Actually the fiero transmissions are strong enough for Tina's sbc.<BR>Last years muncie held up all summer long to Tina daily driver and my occasional (he he) abuse.                                      It wasn't until she decided to park monster for the winter that an abuse test was in order.<BR>After a half hour of pavement painting the unit failed. First the input and intermediate shaft went their separate ways causing<BR>the transmission case to split down the middle, the clutch was still very much intact. With clutch released everything was<BR>smooth, if clutch engaged the car would shake like a dog sh**ing peach pits. The damage to clutch and pressure plate(minus the hot spots) was done racing engine with broken/out of balance input shaft.<P>Eric </B><BR>And yes, plans where to put the Izusu under simular stress test this year,but the unfortunate loss of my torque strut and the salty remains from the last snow storm put a stop to that for this season.<P>Tina  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||sloth85GT||12-09-2001||02:43 AM||||So I think I'll keep my Muncie then.....See how long it lasts.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Archie||12-09-2001||08:32 AM||||Ok....everybody all together now Chant this as loud as you can......<P>"TINA!!!!, TINA!!!!, TINA!!!, TINA!!!, TINA!"<P>Repeat as necessary.<P>You go girl.<P>Archie<BR><P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000021||sloth85GT||12-09-2001||05:01 PM||||And Archie, other than moving the mounts and modifying the transaxles, is there anything else LT1 specific that has to be done for the swap?<P>Also what exactly has to be done to the transaxles? Can it be done by most mechanics or would it be best to send them to you to get done?||||reg||1
Z||000022||sloth85GT||12-12-2001||09:12 AM||||*bump*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||KissMySSFiero||12-12-2001||10:41 AM||||I dont thing you need to modify the transaxle for  the lt1.  you do need to get custom length axles because the engine is shifted to the drivers side of the car.  expect the cost to be a lot higher to do an lt1 because of the axles and the cost of the engine.  <BR>i had thought about doing an lt1 but looked into the cost of an engine.  if you look around and get very lucky you can get a decent used lt1 for $1500-$2000.  but for the same price you can get a new crate motor from gm-$1350,  tpi from ebay or other source $400,  painless wiring harness from jegs-$300 or so.  no it wont have the same power as the lt1, but it can be modified much cheaper and its a new engine.  I have found dozens of used tpi motors in the range of $750 and up depending on milage, what car(corvettes had aluminum heads), and whats on the motor(manifolds, compressors, etc.) <BR>Unless your absolutly stuck on having an lt1 i would say go with the tpi for fuel injection.  its cheaper and much more common, and a lot more simple to work with.  <BR>the reason i wanted to go with the lt1 was because every v8 in the area has tpi.  i want to have somehting different.  but only time(and $$) will tell.<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>Two 88FORMULAS Red & Yellow<BR>        <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>         <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||Archie||12-12-2001||10:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sloth85GT:<BR><B>And Archie, other than moving the mounts and modifying the transaxles, is there anything else LT1 specific that has to be done for the swap?<P>Also what exactly has to be done to the transaxles? Can it be done by most mechanics or would it be best to send them to you to get done?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>One thing we need to make clear here. On the STICK Shift transaxles, I have never modified one and I have not broken one yet. There are no modifications to the stick shift transaxles. On the Automatic transaxles, I recommend that you have it beefed-up by a good transmission shop, As stated many times before, I use JPT transmissions out of Eaton Rapids, MI.<P>The only other LT1 specific thing to note, I mentioned this before, is that using the LT1 in a Fiero you do have to notch the frame rails to make it fit.<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000025||Mach10||12-12-2001||01:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR><B>I dont thing you need to modify the transaxle for  the lt1.  you do need to get custom length axles because the engine is shifted to the drivers side of the car.  expect the cost to be a lot higher to do an lt1 because of the axles and the cost of the engine.  <BR>i had thought about doing an lt1 but looked into the cost of an engine.  if you look around and get very lucky you can get a decent used lt1 for $1500-$2000.  but for the same price you can get a new crate motor from gm-$1350,  tpi from ebay or other source $400,  painless wiring harness from jegs-$300 or so.  no it wont have the same power as the lt1, but it can be modified much cheaper and its a new engine.  I have found dozens of used tpi motors in the range of $750 and up depending on milage, what car(corvettes had aluminum heads), and whats on the motor(manifolds, compressors, etc.) <BR>Unless your absolutly stuck on having an lt1 i would say go with the tpi for fuel injection.  its cheaper and much more common, and a lot more simple to work with.  <BR>the reason i wanted to go with the lt1 was because every v8 in the area has tpi.  i want to have somehting different.  but only time(and $$) will tell.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sloth's mechanic (The one I pointed him too) already has the LT1 pretty much ready to go. It's not a stock 94 (I think?) as it's had some stuff done to it. Current power is sitting at a cool 400HP  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000026||wcapman||12-12-2001||02:30 PM||||I've been running a Getrag on a Northstar since 1998.  My clutch setup is a Quartermaster, 7 1/4" dual disk Pro Street, which is an organic (I think) facing.  This is a light weight assembly that revs like crazy and absolutely positively won't slip overnight.  I previously had a built 2.8 with Nitrous and a Centerforce, that did slip.  I highly recommend the Quartermaster setup for all out performance.  The car runs in the mid 12's.  I have outrun a Viper GTS on the street (close, but a win to 60).  I am constantly running this engine up to the rev limiter (6200)in 1st and 2nd (happens quick).<P>This winter I plan to put on some Paul Vanderley ported heads with Alan Johnson cams.  I plan to replace the old stock Getrag with a newer FWD converted Getrag with a Gleason Torsen limited slip.  Just got the tranny back from the shop all ready to go.  I have put about 10,000 hard miles on a 100,000+ Getrag and it has never had a problem.  It is a little balky shifting into 1st, but it always has been.  I'm going back with a Getrag and I'm going to look for another FWD candidate tranny for a spare JIC.  After the cams and heads are in place I plan to set the rev limiter at 9000.  Then we'll see. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>My only concern with running a manual is that the flywheel is lined up right in the middle of my back.  Anyone given any thought to a scattershield?||||reg||1
Z||000027||California Kid||12-12-2001||04:28 PM||||"wcapman".....At the rpm you're planning to run, a scatter shield should be required as well a loops around both output shafts.  I had gives some earlier thought to putting a scatter shield on mine but backed off, as I'm only running to 6200 max with the TPI. I dont' think it would be too hard to fabricate a scatter shield. You could use a couple of the trans mount studs and a couple brackets to secure it into position. Some high strenght steel with chrome plating would look great to!!<P>Are you running stock CV's??||||reg||1
Z||000028||sloth85GT||12-12-2001||07:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR><B>I dont thing you need to modify the transaxle for  the lt1.  you do need to get custom length axles because the engine is shifted to the drivers side of the car.  expect the cost to be a lot higher to do an lt1 because of the axles and the cost of the engine.  <BR>i had thought about doing an lt1 but looked into the cost of an engine.  if you look around and get very lucky you can get a decent used lt1 for $1500-$2000.  but for the same price you can get a new crate motor from gm-$1350,  tpi from ebay or other source $400,  painless wiring harness from jegs-$300 or so.  no it wont have the same power as the lt1, but it can be modified much cheaper and its a new engine.  I have found dozens of used tpi motors in the range of $750 and up depending on milage, what car(corvettes had aluminum heads), and whats on the motor(manifolds, compressors, etc.) <BR>Unless your absolutly stuck on having an lt1 i would say go with the tpi for fuel injection.  its cheaper and much more common, and a lot more simple to work with.  <BR>the reason i wanted to go with the lt1 was because every v8 in the area has tpi.  i want to have somehting different.  but only time(and $$) will tell.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I spoke with my mechanic and he says the whole swap with labour, exhaust, clutch and eveything will come in under $6000 CDN. He is chargine $3000 CDN for the motor and as Mach10 said, it has an approximate power rating of about 400HP.||||reg||1
Z||000029||artherd||12-13-2001||12:33 AM||||wcapman, we'll have to talk more  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'm working on essentially the same thing, but with their 3-plate SuperPro 7.25". (requres hydraulic TO bearing to fit our cars)<P>My Northstar is the L37 and came out of an ETC Eldorado and has a (stock) red line of '6500' and a hard limiter at 6750...  is yours an LD8?<P>Alan (Chrfab incase anyone here didn't know) does great work, and the motor is amazing.  Want to turn 8,000rpm and make 400bhp?  Replace the lifters and springs and cam.  Done.  <P>Want to make even more? Port heads and   get even stronger springs and Stainless valves.<P><BR>I've been thinking of a scattershield too...  1/2" steel plate...<P>Don't forget to add some driveline 'loops' around your axels, if/when the tranny goes, they will pole-vault into the ground...<P>The clutch should be taking a pretty easy ride here, it's balanced to 15,000 and is rated at over 1,250lb-ft! :P)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/2/nstar-front-on.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/2/nstar-front-on-wide.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
